course: dictionary
lesson: 1305
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which one is meant by reading the words around it, which is the context. «Keep the medicine in the cabinet, high up, away from small children.» You keep medicine inside a cupboard, so here a cabinet is a cupboard.
explain: The same word can mean something quite different. «The cabinet is the top team of ministers; a minister is in charge of one area, like health or education.» Ministers in charge and a team are the clues, so here cabinet means a group of people, not a thing you open.
explain: Ask the question from the last lesson: what would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence. If cupboard makes no sense, try team, then read the sentence again to check. Context clues help you pick the right meaning.
fact: A cabinet is a cupboard with shelves or drawers and usually doors, used to store or show things.
fact: A kitchen cabinet, a bathroom cabinet and a medicine cabinet are all cupboards in a home.
fact: The Cabinet, with a capital C, is the group of the most important ministers who help lead a government and make big decisions.
fact: A display cabinet is a glass cupboard for showing precious things, such as in a museum.
fact: Safety first: keep medicines in a high or locked cabinet, away from small children, and tell a grown-up at once if someone swallows something.
exercise: «The cabinet is the top team of ministers.» In this sentence, what does cabinet mean? | A cupboard for plates | *A team of the most important ministers | A glass case in a museum
exercise: «Keep the medicines in the cabinet, away from small children.» What is a cabinet here? | A glass case for precious things | A team who make big decisions | *A cupboard with doors, for storing things
exercise: Which phrase means a glass cupboard for showing precious things? | Cabinet minister | Kitchen cabinet | Filing cabinet | *Display cabinet
test: «She opened the bathroom cabinet and took out a plaster.» What is a cabinet here? | A museum case | A class of pupils | *A cupboard | A team of ministers
test: «Each leader picks a cabinet, a top team of ministers who run things like health, schools and money.» What does cabinet mean here? | *A team | A wooden shelf for books | A cupboard for cups | A locked drawer for medicine
exam: «ENIAC filled a whole room with cabinets, weighed about 27 tonnes and used about 18,000 glass valves.» What most likely means cabinet here? | A kitchen shelf | A display case for jewels | A team of ministers | *Big cupboard-like boxes that held the machine's parts
